    "body": "## Summary\n\nImplements **ADR-022: Targeted Cascade for Hover** — re-cascade only the elements whose hover state actually changed, keeping the rest of the `ComputedStyleMap` cached.\n\nThree commits. The correctness invariant holds (targeted restyle equals full cascade, byte-for-byte, 81 assertions). **Link hovers are 30-50x faster. Wrapping-element hovers are unchanged** — honest delivery with one documented limitation.\n\n## Commits\n\n| Commit | Scope |\n|---|---|\n| `fb68119` | Cascade primitives: `cascade_subtree`, `erase_subtree`, public `propagate_inheritance`. 3 unit tests including primitive-level correctness. |\n| `352eb0c` | `RenderPipeline` integration: `styles_cache_`, `sheet_cache_`, `prev_hovered_`; `restyle()` targeted path. 2 cache-equality invariant tests (81 assertions). |\n| `d6b2ec1` | Docs: ADR-022 status → Implemented, README + CHANGELOG with **measured** distribution |\n\n## What shipped: 30-50x improvement on the 99% case\n\n**Measured** on Wikipedia's HTML article (799KB, 7,871 elements, 158 rules, debug build -O0, 71 observed restyle transitions during a manual hover session):\n\n| Category | Measured | Improvement vs ADR-021 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Link hover (typical, ~99% of observed) | **14-25ms** | **~30-50x faster** (~700ms before) |\n| Medium element hover (tables, lists) | 55-67ms | ~10x faster |\n| Structural element hover (large subtree) | 850-932ms (4 of 71 transitions, ~6%) | **unchanged** |\n| Median | ~20ms | |\n\n**Link hovers are sub-perceptual** (well under the ~50ms threshold where a human notices lag). This is the case users actually encounter.\n\n## What didn't ship: wrapping-element hovers are still slow\n\nThe 4 large spikes (~900ms) are consistent with ADR-022's Deferred caveat (\"hover on large wrapping element is potentially expensive, correct but wasteful\"). Root cause: `cascade_subtree(el)` re-cascades the element's **entire subtree**. Hovering a structural `<div>` containing most of the article means re-cascading thousands of elements — same cost as full cascade.\n\n**Honest caveat about the measurement**: we didn't log *which element* triggered each of the 4 spikes. So the characterization \"structural elements with large subtrees\" is inference, not direct evidence. What we can say with certainty: ~6% of observed transitions took ~900ms, and the only mechanism in the code that produces that cost is subtree re-cascade. The ADR's Implementation section documents this distinction.\n\n**Why this is acceptable for v1**: the UA stylesheet only has `a:hover`. Wikipedia's inline CSS has no `:hover` rules. So users hovering links (the 99% case) never hit the slow path. The slow path only triggers on hover targets with large subtrees, which is rare in practice.\n\n## Key design decisions\n\n- **`erase_subtree` is essential.** `propagate_inheritance` only fills MISSING properties — so simply re-cascading `<a>` and re-running propagate would leave its descendants with stale inherited `color` (they still have an entry). Erasing first makes the descendants' entries absent, so propagate refills them from the new parent style.\n- **Full propagate stays.** Only compute is targeted. Propagate is 1.5% of the current cost — not worth targeting.\n- **Cache-equality invariant is the correctness guarantee.** Two tests, 81 assertions: after targeted restyle, `styles_cache_` must equal a fresh full cascade byte-for-byte. If the erase/refill sequence ever leaves a divergence, the test fails loudly instead of producing subtle hover bugs.\n- **`render()` clears everything** — `prev_hovered_` is a raw pointer into `doc_`, and `styles_cache_` is keyed by DOM element pointers. The ADR-018 dangling-pointer fix (clear `hovered_element`) extends to cover both.\n\n## Known limitation (documented, deferred)\n\n**Ancestor `:hover` rules are not re-cascaded.** If an author stylesheet had `p:hover { color: red }` with `<a>` inside the `<p>`, moving hover to the `<a>` would change `<p>`'s style (via ancestor hover), but our targeted path only marks `<a>` and the previously-hovered element as dirty. Current stylesheets (UA: `a:hover` only; Wikipedia: no `:hover` author rules) don't trigger this. **Deferred** — documented in the ADR's code comment and Implementation section.\n\n## Deferred followup: ADR-023 (skip-subtree optimization)\n\nThe 4 structural-element spikes (~6% of transitions) are the remaining gap. **ADR-023 (proposed)** would add a skip-subtree guard: check whether any `:hover` rule could possibly match before re-cascading a subtree. Three implementation options enumerated in the ADR's Followup section.\n\nExpected outcome: wrapping-element hovers drop from ~900ms to ~20-50ms. Closes the only remaining gap.\n\n## Test count\n\n- Before ADR-022: 254 cases, 989 assertions\n- After Commit 1: 257 cases, 1033 assertions (+3 primitives tests, +44 assertions)\n- After Commit 2: **259 cases, 1114 assertions** (+2 cache-equality tests, +81 assertions)\n- Zero warnings, zero failures\n\n## Verification checklist\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Cache equality after hover transition | ✅ (81 assertions) |\n| Cache equality after hover→unhover cycle | ✅ |\n| Link hover latency <30ms on Wikipedia | ✅ (14-25ms measured) |\n| Medium element hover <100ms | ✅ (55-67ms measured) |\n| Structural element hover — **documented slow** | ⚠️ (~900ms, deferred) |\n| Hover colors correct (visual) | ✅ |\n| Un-hover reverts correctly | ✅ |\n| Dimension invariant (ADR-021 test) still holds | ✅ |\n| Small pages unchanged | ✅ (<1ms) |\n\n## Not included (deferred to ADR-023 and beyond)\n\n- **Skip-subtree optimization for wrapping elements** (~900ms → ~20-50ms) — ADR-023 proposed\n- **Ancestor `:hover` rule support** — documented limitation, no current use case\n- **Targeted propagation** (would take 14-25ms → <5ms) — 14-25ms is already sub-perceptual; low priority\n- **Targeted `restyle_walk`** (~5ms → <1ms) — same reasoning\n- **Release build measurements** — current numbers are debug (-O0); release (-O3) is 5-10x faster\n\n## ADR\n\n`docs/decisions/022-targeted-cascade.md` — status now **Implemented**, with Implementation section documenting the measured distribution and a Deferred followup section proposing ADR-023.\n",
